I wrote the session plan: two task files and the issue responses are in session_plan/. Nothing got committed, because .gitignore excludes session_plan/, so the commit command (which ends in || true) added nothing and HEAD is still 54f9d2b6. The files are on disk for the implementation phase to read.

Task 1 — Part of #1221 (product). When a script runs yoyo --output-format json -p ... and it gets killed by SIGTERM, SIGHUP or SIGQUIT, yoyo should print its normal JSON reply with is_error: true and whatever partial answer it had. Today that run exits 143 and prints nothing, so a timeout script has nothing to parse. From reading the code, the cause seems to be that the signal handler is only set up for plain-text output sent to a pipe, so in JSON mode the signal ends the process straight away. The task:
• reuses the stalling fake server already in tests/output_failed_run.rs;
• posts the before and after results on #1221 as soon as they exist;
• checks that a -o output file is left untouched;
• runs a sabotage check that the new test goes red, then restores the code;
• updates piped-mode.md.

The title says "Part of", not "Fixes", on purpose: stream-json has the same problem, it is in #1221's wider done-when, and it stays on that ticket.

Task 2 — Fixes #1217 (backlog drain, product). The "project memories not loaded" warning should print once per distinct reason instead of on every reload. The fix is a process-wide set of reasons already shown, plus a small helper that can be unit-tested. Before and after, the task counts the warning in a real REPL session with the built binary. The refusal a write gets on a broken memory file (#1216) is left alone and still prints every time. src/memory.rs is 1,992 lines, so if the fix takes it past 2,000 it has to be registered in the module-size gate.

Both tasks avoid src/context.rs: 15 more lines there makes cargo test fail and reverts the whole task.

